--- abstract

Message size limits mean that large files are sent by email as links
to a web server rather than as attachments.  A link loses the
properties that make an attachment useful: it can stop working, the
file behind it can change, it can't be scanned in transit, and it may
need a separate login.

This document defines the "Content-External" header field, which lets
receiving systems fetch, verify and keep a linked file, and then treat
it like any other attachment.  The field is on a body part with a
fallback link, which aware clients hide.

--- middle

# Introduction {#intro}

Most SMTP systems limit the size of messages that can be sent, which
can be as low as 10 megabytes.  Moreover, limits vary depending on the
recipient's system.  End users want to send files to each other, and
the files they create keep getting bigger.

The usual workaround is to upload the file to a web server and send a
link.  Many clients and services do this, each in their own way.

An attached file has properties that a link lacks:

1. A copy lasts as long as the message.
2. It can't be changed after sending.
3. Systems that handle the message can scan it.
4. Anyone who has the message has the file.

A link can stop working, the file behind it can change, it can't be
scanned in transit, and it may need a separate login.

This document defines the "Content-External" header field
({{header}}), which gives the URL, size and digest of a file and, if
the file is encrypted, its key.  Receiving servers fetch, verify and
scan the file when the message arrives ({{receiving}}), and keep it as
long as the message ({{lifetime}}), which restores those properties.
The field is on a body part with an ordinary link to the file, which
clients that support this document hide ({{fallback}}).

This document replaces {{BIG-FILES-00}}; see {{changes}}.

# Conventions and Definitions

{::boilerplate bcp14-tagged}

This document uses the terms MUA, MDA and message store from
{{MAIL-ARCH}}, and:

sender:
: The sending MUA, or a service acting for it.

linked attachment:
: A file described by a Content-External header field.

attachment server:
: The HTTP {{HTTP}} server that holds a linked attachment.

receiving server:
: Any system on the recipient's side that inspects message content,
  such as an MDA, a content filter or a message store.

# Content-External Header Field {#header}

The generic syntax of the Content-External header field is the same
as that of the Content-Type header field ({{Section 5.1 of MIME}}), so
the same generic parser can be used.  The media type is that of the
linked attachment.  If a different media type is returned when the
linked attachment is retrieved, the value in the header field
overrides it.

The Content-External header field appears in the header section of
the body part that is used to display the message to a user of an MUA
that doesn't support this document ({{fallback}}).  Such body parts
MUST have either the "text/plain" or the "text/html" media type.  A
body part MUST NOT have more than one Content-External header field.
A Content-External header field in any other body part, or in a body
part that has more than one, MUST be ignored.

A linked attachment is treated as if an attached file had been placed
where its body part is in the MIME structure.

The body part's Content-ID ({{Section 7 of MIME}}), if any, identifies
the linked attachment ({{cid-urls}}).

## Parameters {#params}

Parameter names are case-insensitive.  {{MIME-PARAM}} encoding can be
used in parameter values, for long values and for non-ASCII names; it
is decoded before the rules below apply.  Other parameters are
parameters of the media type.

Each parameter below can appear at most once.  A receiver MUST ignore a
Content-External header field in which one appears more than once, a
REQUIRED parameter is missing, or a value is invalid.

URL:
: REQUIRED.  An absolute "https" URI {{URI}} used to access the linked
  attachment ({{fetching}}).

SIZE:
: REQUIRED.  The size of the data in octets, after any decryption.

DIGEST-\<digest-name\>:
: REQUIRED.  A digest of the data, after any decryption.  The digest
  name is from the IANA "Hash Algorithms for HTTP Digest Fields"
  registry {{DIGEST-FIELDS}}, and its value is base64 {{BASE64}}.
  "DIGEST-SHA-256" MUST be present; others MAY be.  Deprecated
  algorithms MUST NOT be used.  Receivers MUST support SHA-256.

STORED-DIGEST-\<digest-name\>:
: OPTIONAL.  A digest of the data as stored at the URL, before
  decryption, with names and values as for "DIGEST-".  It lets a
  recipient check the downloaded data before decrypting it.  It is only
  useful with "KEY".

NAME:
: RECOMMENDED.  The recommended file name of the linked attachment.

DESCRIPTION:
: OPTIONAL.  A description of the linked attachment, as for
  Content-Description ({{Section 8 of MIME}}).

CREATION-DATE, MODIFICATION-DATE, READ-DATE:
: OPTIONAL.  As for Content-Disposition {{CONTENT-DISP}}.

KEY:
: OPTIONAL.  The algorithm, key and nonce used to encrypt the stored
  data ({{key-format}}).  If absent, the data is not encrypted.

EXPIRATION:
: OPTIONAL.  The date-time {{MAIL}} after which the existence of the
  linked attachment is not guaranteed, as for message/external-body
  ({{Section 5.2.3 of MEDIA-TYPES}}).

## Key Format {#key-format}

A key is an algorithm name, the key, and the nonce, separated by
colons.  The key and nonce are base64url {{BASE64}} without padding:

~~~ abnf
key-value       = aead-name ":" key-material ":" nonce
aead-name       = "AEAD_" 1*( ALPHA / DIGIT / "_" )
                  ; name from the AEAD Registry
key-material    = b64url
nonce           = b64url
b64url          = 1*( ALPHA / DIGIT / "-" / "_" )
~~~

The algorithm is named in the IANA AEAD Registry ({{Section 6 of
AEAD}}).  The stored file is the algorithm's ciphertext output, with
empty associated data.  Receivers MUST support "AEAD_AES_128_GCM".
Senders SHOULD use it unless they know every recipient supports
another.

The sender MUST use a new random key for each file.  A file larger than
the algorithm's P_MAX, about 64 GiB for "AEAD_AES_128_GCM", can't be
encrypted this way.

A receiver can't verify the file until it has all of it, and MUST NOT use any of
the plaintext before then.  A receiver that doesn't support the
algorithm treats the linked attachment as unavailable.

## Formal Syntax {#abnf}

This uses ABNF {{ABNF}}.  "type", "subtype", "parameter", "token" and
"value" are from {{MIME}}; CFWS, quoted-string and date-time are from
{{MAIL}}.  All alphabetic characters outside quoted strings are
case-insensitive.

~~~ abnf
content-external = "Content-External:" [CFWS] type "/" subtype
                   *([CFWS] ";" [CFWS] ce-parameter) [CFWS] CRLF

ce-parameter    = url-param / size-param / digest-param /
                  stored-digest-param / name-param /
                  description-param / date-param / key-param /
                  expiration-param / parameter

url-param       = "URL" "=" value      ; absolute "https" URI
size-param      = "SIZE" "=" 1*DIGIT
digest-param    = "DIGEST-" token "=" value   ; base64
stored-digest-param = "STORED-DIGEST-" token "=" value   ; base64
name-param      = "NAME" "=" value
description-param = "DESCRIPTION" "=" value
date-param      = ( "CREATION-DATE" / "MODIFICATION-DATE" /
                    "READ-DATE" ) "=" quoted-string ; date-time
key-param       = "KEY" "=" value      ; key-value
expiration-param = "EXPIRATION" "=" quoted-string ; date-time
~~~

A URL can't contain whitespace, so whitespace in the value of "URL" is
removed before it is used.  This lets a long URL be folded.

## Example {#example-header}

~~~
Content-External: video/mp4; name="site-survey.mp4"; size=734003200;
    description="Drone survey of the north site";
    modification-date="Mon, 16 Nov 2026 17:42:10 +0000";
    url="https://files.example.com/la/
         Hq3Ld9YtW2xKq8vZp0aN5uFbE7cJr1sM";
    digest-sha-256="X48E9qOokqqrvdts8nOJRJN3OWDUoyWxBf7kbu9DBPE=";
    key="AEAD_AES_128_GCM:9Mzuq2AvtfQJxwuIzYB4Gg:Xq3mC8vT0bLw5RzH";
    expiration="Thu, 17 Dec 2026 09:15:00 +0000"
~~~

# Fallback Body Part {#fallback}

The body part with a Content-External header field contains a link from
which the file can be downloaded with a web browser, with its file name
and size.  It SHOULD be text/html {{HTML}}, with "Content-Disposition:
inline", in a multipart/mixed with the message's other parts.  A sender
that knows every recipient's MUA supports this document, for example
because they all use the same service, MAY leave the body part empty.

The download link is usually not the "URL" parameter, which gives the
stored, possibly encrypted, data.  So that the attachment server can
decrypt the file, the download link SHOULD include the key in the
format of {{key-format}}, for example as a query parameter.  The
attachment server chooses the rest of the URL.  See {{security}}.

In a text/html body part, the link SHOULD be inside a "div" element with
class "content-external", so that an MUA that shows several body parts
as one can hide it.

MUAs that support this document SHOULD hide body parts with a
Content-External header field, and show the linked attachment instead.
They MUST NOT hide one unless they have a verified copy of the file.

The example uses the line wrapping of {{WRAP}}:

~~~
=============== NOTE: '\' line wrapping per RFC 8792 ================

Content-Type: multipart/mixed; boundary="b1"

--b1
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8

Here is the survey video.

--b1
Content-Type: text/html; charset=utf-8
Content-Disposition: inline
Content-External: video/mp4; name="site-survey.mp4"; ...

<div class="content-external">
<a href="https://dl.example/pR7wQxV3mTz9KcLe2Hd8Fy?k=AEAD_AES_128_\
  GCM:9Mzuq2AvtfQJxwuIzYB4Gg:Xq3mC8vT0bLw5RzH">site-survey.mp4</a>
(700 MiB)
</div>
--b1--
~~~

Some MUAs show a second text/html body part as an attachment rather
than inline, so the user only sees the link after opening it.

## CID URLs {#cid-urls}

A "cid" URL {{CID}} that refers to the Content-ID of a body part with a
Content-External header field refers to the linked attachment.  For
example, if that body part has "Content-ID:
<a2.20261117T091500Z@mail.example.com>":

~~~ html
<img src="cid:a2.20261117T091500Z@mail.example.com"
     alt="Site plan">
~~~

An MUA resolves such a URL only to a verified copy of the file
({{fetching}}).  An MUA that would have to fetch the file from the
attachment server to display it SHOULD treat it as remote content,
under the same policy as remote images ({{privacy}}).

MUAs that don't support this document resolve these URLs to the
fallback body part, and will show, for example, a broken image.
Senders SHOULD NOT use them unless they know that the recipients' MUAs
support this document.

# Sending {#sending}

## Publishing the File {#publishing}

The sender stores the file on an attachment server, and chooses its
URL and key.  Unless the file is meant to be public, the sender SHOULD
encrypt it ({{key-format}}).

The "URL" and the download link MUST NOT be guessable, unless the file
is already public.  Each SHOULD contain at least 128 random bits, as a
capability URL {{CAPABILITY-URLS}}.

The attachment server MUST serve GET requests for the "URL" without
authentication, cookies or other state, and MUST always return the same
content for it.  It SHOULD keep the file until "EXPIRATION", if given, and
for at least several days in any case.  Its download pages SHOULD send
"Referrer-Policy: no-referrer".

## Composing {#composing}

The MUA adds a body part with a Content-External header field for each
linked attachment ({{fallback}}).  The DKIM {{DKIM}} body hash covers
these fields.

A linked attachment can be kept in a draft, and is published once.

## Forwarding and Replying {#forwarding}

An MUA that forwards a message with linked attachments, or replies and
keeps them, SHOULD publish its own copy of each file ({{publishing}})
and give the new message body parts with Content-External header
fields that point to that copy.  The Content-ID and the parameters
other than "URL", "KEY", "STORED-DIGEST-" and "EXPIRATION" stay the
same.
This keeps the file from depending on the original attachment server
({{security}}).

When forwarding a message as a message/rfc822 body part, which can't be
changed, the MUA SHOULD add body parts with Content-External header
fields for its own copies to the outer message.  Receivers match these to the
encapsulated message's linked attachments by digest, and a receiving
server that has a file with the same digest needn't fetch it again.

# Receiving {#receiving}

## Fetching and Verifying {#fetching}

A receiving server or MUA fetches a linked attachment with a GET
request to its "URL".  The client:

* MUST NOT send cookies, credentials or anything else that identifies
  the recipient;

* MUST verify the server's certificate ({{Section 4.3.4 of HTTP}});

* MAY follow redirects, only to "https" URIs, and SHOULD limit how many;

* treats any final status code other than 2xx as failure, and MAY retry
  after a 429 (Too Many Requests) {{RATE-LIMIT}}, a 5xx or a network
  error; and

* SHOULD stop reading once the content is longer than "SIZE" plus the
  length of the algorithm's tag, if there is a "KEY".

A client that retries MUST back off.  After a response with a
Retry-After header field ({{Section 10.2.3 of HTTP}}), it MUST NOT send
another request to that host before the time given.  Otherwise it MUST
wait at least a minute before the first retry, at least double the wait
after each further failure, and add random jitter.  The back-off
applies to the host, not just the URL, so that a receiving server
fetching many files from one attachment server slows down for all of
them.  A client SHOULD give up after a day or so, and treat the linked
attachment as unavailable.

The client then checks each "STORED-DIGEST-" whose algorithm it
supports, decrypts the file if there is a "KEY", and checks "SIZE" and
each "DIGEST-" whose algorithm it supports.  If no "DIGEST-" is
supported, or any check fails, the linked attachment is unavailable,
and MUST NOT be shown as a verified attachment.

A receiving server MAY decline to fetch a file, for example because it
is too large.  The MUA or the user can then fetch it.

## Content Scanning {#scanning}

A receiving server SHOULD fetch and scan each linked attachment that it
would have scanned if the file had been attached, and apply the
same policy to the result.  If fetching during the SMTP transaction
would take too long, it MAY scan after accepting the message and before
delivering it.

## Storage and Access {#storage}

A message store SHOULD fetch each linked attachment on delivery, if it
wasn't fetched during scanning, and store it with the message.

It SHOULD let the user's MUAs fetch the stored file the same way as an
attached file, so the MUA needn't contact the attachment server.  It
MUST require the user to authenticate, and MUST NOT let a user fetch
the file unless the user can read a message that refers to it.  This
applies to any lookup by digest, and to any proxy for remote content
that the message store offers.

{:aside}
> Open issue: This is specific to each access protocol.  JMAP
> {{JMAP-MAIL}} could list linked attachments among an Email's
> attachments, each with a blobId; {{BLOBEXT}} already describes blobs
> by digest.  IMAP would need a FETCH data item.  These might be
> separate documents, along with a way for an MUA to get proxied remote
> images.

An MUA whose message store doesn't do this MAY fetch the file itself,
when the user opens it or in the background on arrival, but SHOULD NOT
fetch it just because the message was displayed, except for an image
that the user's remote image policy allows ({{cid-urls}}).

## Lifetime {#lifetime}

A message store SHOULD keep a linked attachment as long as any message
that refers to it, and SHOULD remove it once no message does, as it
would an attached file, so that it doesn't keep data the user has
deleted.  It can keep one copy for many messages, found by digest.

# Requirements and Alternatives Not Taken {#alternatives}

The design meets these requirements:

* MUAs that don't support this document can show links to the files.
* A message can have many linked attachments.
* Linked attachments can be kept in drafts, forwarded and replied to.
* Receiving systems can restore the properties in {{intro}}.

The message/external-body media type {{MEDIA-TYPES}} also refers to
external content, but many MUAs show it as an attachment with no link,
and it has no digest or key.

{{BIG-FILES-00}} asked whether the field should also be allowed in the
header section of a message.  This document doesn't allow it, so that
the field always has a fallback, and stays with its body part when the
message is forwarded or restructured ({{Section 5.1 of MEDIA-TYPES}}).

The ExternalPart of {{MIMI-CONTENT}} has much the same information for
instant messaging.  This document uses the same encryption, AEAD
algorithms from the AEAD Registry, and the same option of a digest of
the stored file.  It also requires a digest of the plaintext, so that
copies under different keys match.

# IANA Considerations {#iana}

## Message Header Field {#iana-header}

IANA is asked to add this entry to the "Permanent Message Header Field
Names" registry {{REG-PROC}}:

Header field name:
: Content-External

Applicable protocol:
: mail

Status:
: standard

Author/change controller:
: IETF

Specification document(s):
: this document

## Content-External Parameters {#iana-params}

IANA is asked to create a "Content-External Parameters" registry, with
the Specification Required policy.  Each entry has a name, a
description and a reference.  The initial entries are:

| Name | Description | Reference |
|---|---|---|
| URL | URL of the data | {{params}} |
| SIZE | Size of the data in octets | {{params}} |
| DIGEST-* | Digest of the data | {{params}} |
| STORED-DIGEST-* | Digest of the stored data | {{params}} |
| NAME | Recommended file name | {{params}} |
| DESCRIPTION | Description of the data | {{params}} |
| CREATION-DATE | Creation date of the file | {{params}} |
| MODIFICATION-DATE | Modification date of the file | {{params}} |
| READ-DATE | Last read date of the file | {{params}} |
| KEY | Encryption algorithm, key and nonce | {{params}} |
| EXPIRATION | Date after which the data may be gone | {{params}} |

# Security Considerations {#security}

**Access.**  Anyone with the message can fetch the file, as with an
attachment.  But URLs leak more easily, through browser history, logs
or Referer header fields, so encryption keeps a leaked "URL" alone from
revealing the file.  The download link includes the key, so it is as
sensitive as the message, and the attachment server sees the key when
it is used.  An attachment server could instead put the key in the
fragment and decrypt in the browser.

**Guessable URLs.**  Even if the file is encrypted, a guessable URL
reveals that it exists and its size.

**Changed content.**  Whoever controls the URL can change the file
without changing the message, and the digest detects this.  The digest
is required even with "KEY", because every recipient has the key, and
so does the attachment server once the download link is used, so any
of them can make a ciphertext that decrypts correctly.

**Request forgery.**  Receiving servers MUST NOT fetch from addresses
that aren't publicly routable, such as loopback, link-local or private
addresses, and MUST check again after each redirect.

**Resource use.**  Receiving servers SHOULD limit the number and total
size of linked attachments they fetch per message.

**Denial of service.**  Someone who receives a message with linked
attachments can resend it, or just its Content-External header fields,
to millions of recipients, each of whose servers then fetches the file
from the original attachment server.  The same works with a "URL" that
points at any large file on any server.  Forwarding MUAs that publish
their own copies ({{forwarding}}) avoid this for ordinary forwarding.
Receiving servers SHOULD fetch a URL only once, and reuse the result
for every message that names the same URL and digest, and SHOULD limit
how fast they fetch from any one host.  Attachment servers MAY limit how
often they serve a file, and receivers MUST back off when they do
({{fetching}}).

**Misleading links.**  The fallback link might not match the header
field.  An MUA that hides them and shows the verified file avoids
this.

**Scanning.**  The key is in the message, so systems that handle the
message can decrypt the file.  Encryption protects it only from the
attachment server, unless the download link is used, and from anyone
with the URL alone.

# Privacy Considerations {#privacy}

The attachment server sees each fetch.  If an MUA fetches the file
from it when the user views the message or opens the file, the
sender learns when and from where, as with a remote image, and
per-recipient URLs show who.  An image shown through a "cid" URL
({{cid-urls}}) is then a "web bug" ({{Section 9.9 of E2E-GUIDANCE}}).
Fetching on arrival ({{storage}}) shows only that the message was
delivered, and hides the recipient's address.

